8. What is Bounded Context?----------------------------------check again
"A Bounded Context is a clearly defined boundary within a business domain where a particular domain model, terminology, and business rules have a specific meaning. For example, in a banking system, Account Management, Payment Processing, and Loan Management can be different bounded contexts. The term Account may have different meanings and rules in each context. Bounded Contexts help us avoid a large shared domain model and help us identify appropriate boundaries for microservices."


9. What is Domain-Driven Design (DDD)?
   Domain-Driven Design, or DDD, is a software design approach where we design the application around the business domain, business concepts, and business rules.
For example, in a banking application, concepts such as Customer, Account, Transaction, Payment, and Loan represent different parts of the business domain.
In microservices, DDD helps us identify business boundaries called bounded contexts. Each bounded context can become a separate microservice with its own business logic and data ownership.
The main goal is to keep the software model closely aligned with the actual business requirements and make the system easier to understand and evolve.


