"Each microservice should ideally have its own database to maintain data ownership and loose coupling. This allows services to be independently developed, deployed, and scaled, and prevents one service from becoming tightly coupled to another service's database schema. If one service needs another service's data, it should communicate through APIs or events rather than directly accessing the other service's database."

2. What problems occur when multiple services share one database?
"When multiple microservices share the same database, they become tightly coupled to the database schema. Schema changes require coordination between multiple teams and can prevent independent deployments. It also creates a shared scalability and performance bottleneck because all services compete for the same database resources. In addition, one service's heavy queries or failures can affect other services, and services have less flexibility to choose the appropriate database technology."

