2. Why can't we achieve all three simultaneously?
Because during a network partition, Consistency and Availability conflict with each other.
"We cannot guarantee all three because when a network partition occurs, the system has to choose between consistency and availability. If we continue accepting requests, we may return or create conflicting data, sacrificing consistency. If we reject or wait for requests until the nodes can communicate, we preserve consistency but sacrifice availability. Therefore, during a partition, we choose either CP or AP."

3. What is Eventual Consistency?
Eventual consistency means that after a data update, different replicas may temporarily have different values, but if no new updates occur and the system continues operating, all replicas will eventually become consistent.

4. Strong Consistency vs Eventual Consistency?
After a successful write, any subsequent read returns the latest value--Strong Consistency
Eventual Consistency
After a write, some replicas may temporarily return an older value, but eventually all replicas converge to the latest value.

