18. What is Distributed Tracing?
Distributed tracing is used to track a single request as it travels across multiple microservices. A trace is made up of spans representing individual operations in different services. It helps us identify where failures or performance bottlenecks occur. We propagate a Trace ID across service calls so that all the spans belonging to the same request can be correlated."

19. Why is correlation ID important?
"In a distributed system, a single client request can travel through multiple microservices, making it difficult to trace the request when an issue occurs. We generate a unique Correlation ID and propagate it through the request, usually using an HTTP header. Each service includes this ID in its logs. We can then search for that ID across centralized logs and trace the complete request flow and identify where the failure occurred."

A Correlation ID is a unique identifier assigned to a client request and propagated across multiple services so that we can trace the entire request flow through the distributed system.


